				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/deltathree" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6974566); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6974566&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609559558" alt="DeltaThree" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6974566)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/deltathree" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6974566); return false;" >DeltaThree</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608785988 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Dec 2020 04:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852441">
									

<p>From what I've read, it was a good idea that was taken out of hand. We're trying to give it another shot with Alpha-9, but the key is, we need to stop thinking X-Men and more Suicide Squad. When I first got here and found out about these MTFs, my thinking was that it is very hard to contain all these things and if some are useful, use them, and if some are people who may be cooperative, make them earn their keep. The idea should be SCPs are being put to work because it's expensive to house and feed them so the least they can do is help when we need them, assuming they will help at all. But it turns into a Mary-Sue fest because people want to make superheroes from anomalies instead of considering SCPs as horrors first and a last resort to solve other problems when nothing else works. Omega-7 went off the rails and the authors put the rabid beast down, and if we are going to give it a second shot, make sure the writers know the idea is anomalies that got caught and are out on work release.</p>

							</div>

								
					
